A||N||32||bcwawright||Venting Air From Radiator Area||||5||
Z||000000||bcwawright||05-25-2001||10:31 PM||||Has anyone done any type of R&D to eliminate the tremendous air pressure buildup at the radiator area when driving at high speed?<BR>It seems that putting vents on the front hood area would upset the air flow over the hood thereby reducing downforce also. Dropping a portion of the hood down like a scoop would relieve the air pressure buildup and still give the frontal area for adequate downforce.<BR>Has anyone tried modifying the hood in this manner? Does anyone have any solutions or suggestions?||||reg||5
Z||000001||sjp777||05-25-2001||10:55 PM||||ummm, yeah. It's been done and discussed by dozens if not hundreds on this forum.<P>do a search for hood scoops or hood vents. I'm sure you'll find everything you need, including pictures<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/890sm_2.jpg">   <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/</A>||||reg||1
Z||000002||gixxer||05-25-2001||11:30 PM||||Nobody on this forum has modified their Fiero in the manner you described (that I'm aware of, anyway) . A few people have only performed a rather weak vent modification to shunt a little bit of air out the top to keep the headlight doors from popping open at speed, rather than a full fledged aero duct job. <BR>The downforce you would get with your mod is anybodys guess, but it could be substantial.<BR>The big drawbacks could be high speed oversteer, and lots of hot air in your face with the windows and sunroof open.<BR>Good luck in whatever you decide to do.<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||rockcrawl||05-25-2001||11:47 PM||||You mean like this? Nah, it's never been done  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/DCP01093.jpg"> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/DCP01095.jpg"> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/DCP01091.jpg"> <P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT (driver)<BR>'86 SE (4.5 PFI V8 transplant victim)<BR>'84 coupe (parts)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||7
Z||000004||bcwawright||05-25-2001||11:59 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rockcrawl:<BR><B>You mean like this? Nah, it's never been done   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> <P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/DCP01093.jpg">  <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/DCP01095.jpg">  <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/DCP01091.jpg">  <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Did you make the molds for this? Looks really good.......did you notice any difference on the front end at high speeds?<BR>Thanks for the pics!<P>Bruce<BR>88GT Turbo Spyder F355<P>||||reg||10
Z||000005||sjp777||05-26-2001||12:09 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by gixxer:<BR><B>Nobody on this forum has modified their Fiero in the manner you described (that I'm aware of, anyway) . </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>??????????????<P>I must spend too much time in here. I can't count as many times as I've seen that mod on this forum||||reg||1
Z||000006||rockcrawl||05-26-2001||12:18 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Did you make the molds for this? Looks really good.......did you notice any difference on the front end at high speeds?<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Yes I made a mold for the vent, but I have no time to make more. I still don't have one on my personal car, but the guy who drives the yellow car said it does make a difference. I'm not sure how serious he was though. Theoreticly it <B>should</B> work, and if you like the way it looks, there's nothing to loose.<P>Jon<BR>||||reg||7
Z||000007||sjp777||05-26-2001||12:27 AM||||Took a little archive searching, but I found the one I was thinking of.<P>Go Here:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fortunecity.com/silverstone/impala/403/fieroparts.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fortunecity.com/silverstone/impala/403/fieroparts.htm</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||gixxer||05-26-2001||11:20 AM||||That's correct "it's never been done".<BR>The venting shown is what I was referring to as "weak". By saying weak, I mean watered down from what I think it should or could be.<BR>I thought bcawright was describing a more radical and functional ducting arraingment similar to a Ford GT40 Mk 4/ McLaren Can-Am car.<BR>Downforce fron the vents shown? I don't think so. A little less lift perhaps.<BR>To get real downforce you would have to vent the ENTIRE radiator outflow UP thru the hood area in front of the windshield. <BR>I was pretty disappointed when I saw this more cosmetic mod in person at a Fiero gathering as it seems to be a half-heated attempt to look racy and doesn't really address the downforce issue.<BR>At work we would call it a "non-value-added" modification. <BR>It does look nice 'tho.<BR>My apologies for wasting peoples time.<BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Songman||05-26-2001||12:04 PM||||Hey Gixxer,<P>I'm sure no one thinks you are wasting our time here... This is all about sharing our views...<P>The thing that comes to mind for me here is that we are not talking about a car that is going to go 150+ mph.<P>The vents as shown are functional and do serve a purpose. Anyone who has these will tell you that the biggest benefit of them is that your headlight doors don't flap at high speed anymore, and yes, some amount of air is vented out of the radiator.<P>You also have to remember how a Fiero is supposed to cool. The vents above the engine are not intakes, but exhaust. Air from under the car comes up and out of them... We know that air under a car is not a good thing at high speeds but we are not talking about that kind of speeds from our little cars.<P>If someone wanted to go all out and vent all of the radiator air out through the hood, drop and dam the car to keep air from underneath, and put functional vents on both sides to supply the air to help vent the engine it would certainly be cool.. But I think the cost would certainly outweigh the performance improvement.<P>I'm no engineer but that is how I understand it. Anyone with comments?<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif">  <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A><p>[This message has been edited by Songman (edited 05-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||rockcrawl||05-26-2001||11:21 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>I was pretty disappointed when I saw this more cosmetic mod in person at a Fiero gathering as it seems to be a half-heated attempt to look racy and doesn't really address the downforce issue.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR> I'm pretty sure you have not seen one of mine in person, unless you drove 2800 miles to go to a Fiero gathering. Every one I have ever made at this point is right here in PA, and every one of them is functional. There are two 1.5" x 7" openings directly above the radiator. How much does it actually do? Who knows, I'm not about to spend the money and time to research it. It does keep the headlight doors from lifting at high speeds.<BR>  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Downforce from the vents shown? I don't think so. A little less lift perhaps.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR> Doesn't reduced lift have the same effect as increased downforce? How can "vent(ing) the ENTIRE radiator outflow UP thru the hood area in front of the windshield" increase downforce? Wouldn't that just reduce lift? I really don't see a difference between the two terms. I don't know, I'm not an engineer either, I just make "weak" hood vents.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR> Lets face it, the spoiler on the back of a Fiero is at least 95% cosmetic. The wing on the back of a Countach was a $5000 option that made the car <B>slower</B>. If we find something that we think looks good, even if it provides little or no performance improvement, it does add value. That is unless you think it's ugly.<BR>I'd like to see what a larger GT-40 type vent would look like on a Fiero. I may have to play around with photoshop a bit. On thing is for sure, you can't make it any bigger than mine without cutting into the spare tire/sunroof storage area. One of my guidelines was to keep the weatherstrip there untouched.<P>I'm not trying to start a rumble or anything, just stating my opinions. gixxer, please don't take this as an attack at you, I'm just trying to make friendly discussion. The last thing we need here is another flame-war, but a controlled debate is good.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/DCP01093.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||DKOV||05-27-2001||12:12 AM||||I race my Fiero (88 GT) at PIR for fun and Solo comps...  At the end of the front straight, nearly 140mph, I don't see my headlamps popping up or any susbstantial "buffering" of air...<P>Am I just not going fast enough or am I missing something?<P>I only ask because I am just finishing up an engine swap to a 3.4L Dual Twin Cam that has been tweaked a bit to nearly 300 HP.  The first thing I was planning was a few "hot laps" around the road course.<P>Should I be worried here?<P>DKOV -||||reg||1
Z||000012||Formula88||05-27-2001||12:17 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by gixxer:<BR><B>That's correct "it's never been done".<BR>The venting shown is what I was referring to as "weak". By saying weak, I mean watered down from what I think it should or could be.<BR>I thought bcawright was describing a more radical and functional ducting arraingment similar to a Ford GT40 Mk 4/ McLaren Can-Am car.<BR>Downforce fron the vents shown? I don't think so. A little less lift perhaps.<BR>To get real downforce you would have to vent the ENTIRE radiator outflow UP thru the hood area in front of the windshield. <BR>I was pretty disappointed when I saw this more cosmetic mod in person at a Fiero gathering as it seems to be a half-heated attempt to look racy and doesn't really address the downforce issue.<BR>At work we would call it a "non-value-added" modification. <BR>It does look nice 'tho.<BR>My apologies for wasting peoples time.<BR> </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Well, by definition, reducing lift IS increasing downforce.  It is fully function, albiet not a functional as a race-only inspired design that doesn't take asthetics into consideration.  Not a flame, just an observation.<P>That's why the 2nd generation TA's had fender vents - same thing.  As for the Countach spoiler - yeah, it slows the car, cuz the downforce it creates causes drag.  Everything is a tradeoff.  Now the spoiler on my Formula is almost completely useless aerodynamically.  In the rain, water on the spoiler hardly moves.  It's not in enough of the airstream to make a difference one way or the other.||||reg||1
Z||000013||sjp777||05-27-2001||12:45 AM||||And another<BR> <A HREF="http://www.v8archie.com/warberhood.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.v8archie.com/warberhood.htm</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000014||gixxer||05-27-2001||01:32 AM||||The prototype Fiero in 1984 or whatever had 120 lbs of lift at 100 MPH. <BR>A Fiero weighs about 1200 lbs at the front, at rest. <BR>So if you were to run it up to 100 MPH this same Fiero would weigh 1080 lbs on the front wheels.<BR>This is aerodynamic lift.<BR>If you were to shunt the same volume of air out the top of the hood thru a rather large hole, possibly the same area as the radiator, with ductwork affixed to the back of the radiator and thru the hood, then it is possible, depending on the surface area of the backside of the duct, and the weight of the air being deflected UP, to get a DOWNFORCE on the front tires.<BR>Lets pick 120 lbs.<BR>Which would increase the weight on the front tires to say, 1400 lbs.<BR>The car effectively weighs more because of DOWNFORCE.<BR>Thereby increasing  the cornering power of the front end.<BR>I have no objection to the tiny little vents to keep  the headlights covers from popping up. <BR>I even think they look GOOD.<BR>But they only serve to vent a SMALL part of the radiator air, and cannot possibly generate downforce by themselves.<BR>The Fiero lends itself very well to ducting the air ejected by the radiator COMPLETELY out the top of the hood, using the space currently occupied by the spare tire.<BR>I thought I pointed this out well enough out in prior posts that NOBODY has done this before. Guess I didn't do a good enough job.<BR>I can't understand why everybody has such a blind spot about this!<BR>Don't you guys look at race cars and wonder why they do things the way they do?<BR>There are even books on the subject that tell you have to fabricate these ducts.<BR>Geesh!<BR>This is my last post on this subject.<BR>I'm sick of it.<BR> <BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000015||sjp777||05-27-2001||02:01 AM||||OK OK, don't get your panties in a wad.<P>I think the purpose of the question asked by the first post was how to eliminate the build up of pressure that causes the front end to come up and the headlight doors to flap at high (120+) speed. The vents shown here accomplish that, as well as substantially aiding in engine cooling, while still giving roughly the same surface area for downforce. He didn't ask how to increase downforce, just how to avoid losing any.<P>Not too many people are looking for ways to make the Fiero turn hard at 120. If you're doing that speed in these cars, it'd better be in as straight a line as possible.<P>So relax, nobody's saying you don't have a valid point, we just have different perceptions of the question posed.<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/890sm_2.jpg">    <BR>88GT 5sp, in desperate need of an LT1<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/</A>||||reg||1
Z||000016||Songman||05-27-2001||02:23 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by gixxer:<BR><B>Don't you guys look at race cars and wonder why they do things the way they do?<P>This is my last post on this subject.<BR>I'm sick of it.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>This is my last post on it because I'm sick of it too! heheh<P>How many people here are building race cars out of their Fieros? Raise your hands...<P>One, two, three.. not very many...<P>Yes, I look at race cars and wonder why they do what they do. As a matter of fact, you can find a couple of Dale Jarrett Fords parked in my driveway every few weeks.. but never one time have I thought "Well, why don't I do that to my Fiero that I drive on the street everyday?"<P>I'm not trying to be a smart ass, but dude, lighten up a little bit... This is supposed to be fun. We don't always agree here, but that's okay... we're all friends.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000017||Oreif||05-27-2001||02:40 AM||||You are right, The tiny vents do not create downforce. BUT, They do reduce the amout of lift. By relieving the pressure built up above the radiator you decrease the amount of lift. So going by your example of 120lbs of lift, let say the ducts reduce that by 40. So now you have only 80lbs of lift instead of 120lbs. <P>bcwawright is looking to relieve the pressure WITHOUT reducing downforce. It doesn't sound like he wants to add downforce.<P>Here's a pic of my hood. <P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/hood_dt_1.jpg">  <P>------------------<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">     <BR><B><I>Got Emblems?</I></B><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 05-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000018||Nashco||05-27-2001||02:47 AM||||Well, I've been watching this for a while, and was hoping there WAS somebody who had built it.  Lots of people have thought about it, they just haven't realized it.  The hood vents are doing the same thing (changing downforce), just not to an extreme.  I was thinking about this the other day when I had my spare tire out, seeing what all was in the way.  I am a long ways from doing 130 mph jaunts because my suspension needs rebuilt, but I was thinking about fooling around with this someday.<P>Here's an example of some stuff that uses this method:<P><A HREF="http://www.supercars.net/SDBQ?y=1999&m=Pagani&o=Zonda%20C12-S" TARGET=_blank>Panoz Zonda</A><BR><A HREF="http://www.supercars.net/SDBQ?y=1995&m=Ford&o=GT90%20Concept" TARGET=_blank>Ford GT90</A><BR><A HREF="http://www.supercars.net/SDBQ?y=2001&m=Lotus&o=Elise" TARGET=_blank>Lotus Elise</A><P>The GT90 and Elise are pretty mainstream, as far as being recognizable and admired by lots of people.  This idea isn't strictly race car, it's strictly mid/rear engine.  If there were more mid/rear engined cars out there, this would be common practice.  Mid/rear engine cars tend to be a little light in front, or perfectly balanced.  When you're running at high speeds, you want all the steering control you can get.<P>If you removed all the obstructions in the spare tire area, added some very very low ground effects, added an air dam that was right on the ground (obviously made of some durable material), and reworked the engine bay cooling method, only then would this be possible.  These things are all pretty basic, except for reworking the engine bay cooling method.  You would have to use some major scoop-age to get good airflow through there.  But, if you took care of all the above mentioned items, it wouldn't be too hard to create this effect.<P>I am hoping that nobody actually puts this idea into play before me.  I'm probably shooting myself in the foot by posting, but I'd like somebody to do a little R&D along with or before me.  My biggest concern is engine bay cooling, because that will require several fiberglass pieces that I don't want to pay for or design.  This is definitely a project on the very farthest away back-burner, but something that I will do eventually.<P>If anybody has some start on this project, drawings, ideas, additions, negative thoughts, warnings, etc. on this project, I'd like to hear what you have to say.  This is a brainstorming thread, and two minds is just a start, ten would be better <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000019||Philphine||05-27-2001||09:04 AM|||| <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/004850.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/004850.html</A> <P>i got this car from fierodave but i haven't done much with it yet. that whole shaded area in front is open but the hood flexes so much that a lot of the louvers are lose. also the front trunk wall was kind of bent back so i'm not real sure of how well it would seal and it won't hold a spare i think (haven't had one in it yet). fierodave may have more detailed pics still (couldn't find the rest that were on the net before). or possibly if i can take the count up on his offer to come by and snap some. i'm not any closer to being able to than i've ever been.<P>i did find all my regular cameras (i tend to buy them for guard trips then come home and lose them) and i've taken some i could snail.||||reg||1
Z||000020||Nashco||05-27-2001||04:24 PM||||Oh Philphine!  That is so wrong!  Well, it's the most basic form of what I'm trying to show....but so so wrong!<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||10
Z||000021||Pontiaddict||05-27-2001||06:06 PM||||I thought that the original question was about how to get the air out from under the hood without adding drag by disrupting airflow over the car. I odn't think that the vents disrupt the air enough to increase drag.<P>Other suggestions to reduce lift is to vent the underhood air to cool the brakes, add side vents to extract the air. Direct the airflow to produce a Venturri effect that will extract more air, and add a fan to evacuate air from the engine compartment. some will be better to implement than others.||||reg||1
Z||000022||ray b||05-27-2001||07:13 PM||||wheels with a fan shaped action like the 16" vetts in early 84 or about.or fans to dump air out in to wheel wells.but vett wheels they don't fit unless you do diffrent brakes and hubs<BR>but lift and down force are diffrent things one is up the other down, than theres drag  max the downforce but keep drag at a mini to go fast<BR>a bigger rubber strip under front chin is cheapest airo fix for less drag and lift.<BR>do the airo front [indy or gt] work or just for looks??<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000023||Pontiaddict||05-27-2001||07:37 PM||||The GT aero package is good for reducing the drag coefficient form .377 to .350. For the notchback anyway. The rear wing helps with this number as well.||||reg||1
Z||000024||Bazooka||05-27-2001||08:14 PM||||  I built a 'Flow Thru' style hood with an L-88 style scoop on top. It was obvious that stability improved greatly above 50 mph as there was a more solid response to steering, etc. The vent sizing was made large enough to where the inner containment wall for the spare tire needed no modification and the relief vents had a sloped entrance on top. The venting also let the engine run just a bit cooler too, something that is needed on a V8. Check my webpage for a couple of photos of the hood. Its at     <A HREF="http://hometown.aol.com/m34chev/myhomepage/auto.html" TARGET=_blank>http://hometown.aol.com/m34chev/myhomepage/auto.html</A> <P>************************************** <BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Pontiaddict:<BR><B>I thought that the original question was about how to get the air out from under the hood without adding drag by disrupting airflow over the car. I odn't think that the vents disrupt the air enough to increase drag.<P>Other suggestions to reduce lift is to vent the underhood air to cool the brakes, add side vents to extract the air. Direct the airflow to produce a Venturri effect that will extract more air, and add a fan to evacuate air from the engine compartment. some will be better to implement than others.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000025||ray b||05-28-2001||08:27 PM||||airo nose has less drag? ok but what about lift? and no way will any wing lessen drag<BR>noway no how it must ADD DRAG to make downforce and even if it don't make any downforce it will still drag some.lip spoiller may cut drag some but not a wing.<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000026||V8Dreamer||05-28-2001||10:22 PM||||Well, I AM the guy who drives the yellow beat up old 2m4 in Rockcrawl's pics..... I like the hood mostly because of cosmetic reasons, but I will say that it definately does serve some other purposes.  I noticed that my car runs 10 - 15 degrees cooler than it did without the vents, and also it is much less flighty at higher speeds.  And Gixxer, unless you have seen my car in person, you have never seen THIS particular mod....I am the first and only one so far using Rockcrawl's vent molds.<P>If anyone has any questions, you can E-mail me at ensoniq322@hotmail.com<P>Ryan||||reg||1
Z||000027||cadaver||05-28-2001||11:07 PM||||So Gixxer you are planning on going 140 AND cornering? That should be some car you are building.||||reg||1
Z||000028||onfire||05-28-2001||11:58 PM|||| <A HREF="http://www.mindspring.com/~martinwhite/fiero/hood.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.mindspring.com/~martinwhite/fiero/hood.html</A> <P>||||reg||1
Z||000029||Nashco||05-29-2001||02:33 AM||||Sheesh, where the hell did all this tension come from?  Chill out guys...<P>Onfire, I've seen that website before, I really like that mod.  It looks slick and is pretty cheap too.  Oh, and no cheesy extra molds are required <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Cadaver...it's not that we're planning on "cornering" at high speeds, but the faster you're going, the more control is desired.  That's the nice thing about the flow-through radiator/body designs of those cars I pointed out earlier.  At lower speeds, they don't have any noticeable "drag" effects because there isn't enough flow to create a downforce.  Once you get into the triple digit range, some real downforce is created to increase handling ability.  For some this may be too touchy, but most of us who can control our cars well desire all the sensitivity we can get.<P>Personally, I wish my car had all the sensitivity, oversteer, overbraking, overpowering, accelerating ability it can handle.  As a driver, I can control how I want to use all of that.  If I happen to do 110 someday, for one reason or another, I want to be DAMN sure I can steer if I have to all of the sudden.<P>Anybody ever see that crash video of the Corvette trying to outrun the cops, but lost control at about 135 mph?  I bet he wishes he had some "drag" that was creating downforce right about then <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Oh, and seriously, don't get too touchy guys; they're just words, and we all have our own ideas/ideals/opinions.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000030||Formula88||05-29-2001||08:52 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DKOV:<BR><B>I am just finishing up an engine swap to a 3.4L Dual Twin Cam that has been tweaked a bit to nearly 300 HPDKOV -</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Ok, give...what did you do to get 300 HP out of a 3.4L TDC?  Inquiring minds want to know!<BR>||||reg||10
Z||000031||DKOV||05-29-2001||05:21 PM||||Well... <P>Keep in mind, I havn't dyno'd it in the Fiero yet but in the GP it topped 260 BEFORE many of the internal mods I made during the swap to the Fiero.  I might be a bit optimistic, but I am sure that I have added AT LEAST 30 more morses if not closer to 50.  I'll re-post AFTER I dyno it and perhaps have some timeslips to support  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>So...  I can't take ALL the credit.  Some of it was done by the previous owner of the original Grand Prix GTP I bought.<P>He is/was a track official at PIR when I bumped into him and he had done some modifications he described to me as "stroked" .  As you say, inquiring minds want to know so I checked a bit further...<P>Apparently he bored over a few mills, did a valve & cam job and lengthened the stroke some too.  The only physically measurable difference I can perform is the addition of the oversized oil pan and an extra 1.25 quarts of oil needed to fill it.<P>Above that, there are the usual mods such as the K&N filter/charger, manifold spacer & tornado, 2.5inch custom exhaust (headers, cat, 2 glass packs and manza tips), 3mm bored out throttle body, polished heads (in's and out's), CPU upgrade, valve work, opened up MAF sensor, underdrive crank pulley, etc.<P>I'm concerned because at first, I heard that the 282 tranny can handle the HP and more to 350 or so.  But then I've heard that 300 is pushing it.  <P>I have picked up a torbo setup following Dennis Lagrua's concept for his 3.4 push rod... I'm not sure I want to add it now.  Of course, the Turbo and supporting accesories only cost me $50 at a U-Pull-It yard and another $75 for a rebuild kit so if I scrap the project, it's not a HUGE loss.<P>I've also got an MSD-6 adjustable Ignition system for it that I planned on adding after the Turbo install.  I suppose I should just stick that in there now  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I am ALWAYS looking for more HP so if any of you can think of some more "tweaks" to squeeze out another few ponies...<P>I'm all ears!<P>DKOV -||||reg||1
Z||000032||lowCG||05-29-2001||10:44 PM||||I made an opening(5"X21")behind/above the radiator and my headlight doors still open at slightly higher speeds.<BR>I think the opening in front of the radiator would need to be made alot larger along with a smoother exit behind to even begin to think about these cars being serious high speed contenders(helping to keep the airflow from going under the nose).<BR>I already have a fabricated pan under the nose,maybe I could add on some bubble-fairings to help get the air around the tires...||||reg||1
